      "provenance": {
        "notes": "Look-alikes and name clash: many Thymus species; T. serpyllum is the creeping one and T. cilicicus is less hardy (RHS). This entry is common thyme, T. vulgaris. Watering: RHS says rarely needs water except newly planted or in containers in hot dry weather, overwatering and winter damp rot the roots; NC State genus page says intolerant of wet soils and poor drainage. let_fully_dry is the agent's mapping; interval 10 days is a guess. Pots: at least 15 cm wide and 10 cm deep, soil-based compost with up to 25 percent grit. Light: both say full sun. Size: RHS says small mounded shrubs up to 30 cm for T. vulgaris and plant spacing 20 to 30 cm; spread 30 is taken from the spacing, hence from_knowledge_verify. Growth rate slow is the NC State genus reading, applied to the species. Hardiness: RHS most widely sold varieties withstand frost and UK winters; NC State genus page USDA zones 5a to 9b; no temperature stored. Toxicity: ASPCA lists T. vulgaris non-toxic to cats and dogs; the NC State genus page has no pet tags. Severity 0 is our grading. Es and it names are machine drafts."
      },
